# Patch 0087 — Power-user tooling (ROADMAP Phase 7: "capture, annotation,
# citations, REST client, JSON viewer, writing mode").
#
# THE FIRST THING THIS PATCH DID WAS AUDIT THE LIST, and half of it turned out
# to be already shipped or already someone else's job. Recorded here so the
# roadmap line stops reading as six missing features:
#
#   capture      — Firefox ships Screenshots and it is ON in this build
#                  (screenshots.browser.component.enabled). Kavacha adds a
#                  palette entry that calls it. A reimplementation would have
#                  been a second, worse screenshot tool.
#   annotation   — shipped in patch 0082: highlights with comments.
#   JSON viewer  — Firefox's is on by default (devtools.jsonview.enabled=true
#                  in all.js, verified against this build's engine tree, not
#                  assumed). Nothing to build and nothing to pin; a redundant
#                  pref would only be one more thing to keep true.
#   citations    — genuinely missing. Built here.
#   writing mode — genuinely missing. Built here.
#   REST client  — genuinely missing and deliberately NOT built. It is a
#                  developer tool with its own request store, auth handling
#                  and history, which is a product inside a product; the
#                  roadmap already calls this row a marketplace-bundle
#                  candidate, and this is the item that row was about. Left
#                  open in REMAINING_WORK rather than half-built in core.
#
# CITATIONS READ WHAT THE PAGE DECLARES ABOUT ITSELF. Scholarly sites publish
# citation_* meta tags and most others publish OpenGraph, so the metadata
# comes through the indexer actor (one more narrow query on the same content
# channel, rather than a second way for chrome to touch a page), with the tab
# title and URL as the fallback that always works. Best effort, and honest
# where it matters: with no author declared, APA correctly leads with the
# TITLE rather than inventing "Anonymous" — the rule people most often get
# wrong by hand, which is half the reason this exists — and the BibTeX key is
# derived from host+year, because an author is the field most often missing
# and a key of "undefined2026" is worse than one that is merely unlovely.
# TeX syntax characters in a title are escaped, so a page called "Cost & Risk"
# does not silently break someone's build hours later.
#
# It is NOT a reference manager: no library, no BibTeX database, no author
# de-duplication. Kavacha's answer to "manage my references" is Zotero, which
# the Student template already recommends (patch 0053). This is the
# thirty-second job that sending someone to a reference manager makes worse.
#
# ONE COMMAND WITH A PROMPT, not three palette rows. Three near-identical
# entries is how a palette stops being worth opening; the chosen style becomes
# the default, so the second citation is one keystroke.
#
# WRITING MODE IS A VIEW, NOT A STORE. about:write edits notes that already
# exist — the active Space's note (patch 0008) or one page's note (patch
# 0082, via ?url=) — so there is never a copy of the user's words that only
# writing mode knows about. That is precisely the failure that makes
# "distraction-free editors" bolted onto other apps untrustworthy. It is
# Reader Mode pointed the other way: one tab, one column, nothing else on
# screen, Escape to leave.
#
diff --git a/locales/en-US/browser/browser/zen-command-palette.ftl b/locales/en-US/browser/browser/zen-command-palette.ftl
index b4507d6..8ac47fd 100644
--- a/locales/en-US/browser/browser/zen-command-palette.ftl
+++ b/locales/en-US/browser/browser/zen-command-palette.ftl
@@ -164,3 +164,10 @@ kavacha-action-open-workflows = Workflows
 # Kavacha patch 0086 — the tab history tree and the saved-session manager.
 kavacha-action-tab-tree = Tab History Tree
 kavacha-action-saved-sessions = Saved Sessions
+
+# Kavacha patch 0087 — power-user tooling. "Capture This Page" drives
+# Firefox's own Screenshots component: the browser already ships the feature,
+# and reimplementing it would be a second, worse one.
+kavacha-action-copy-citation = Copy Citation
+kavacha-action-screenshot = Capture This Page
+kavacha-action-writing-mode = Writing Mode
